<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick William, Duke of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1771–1815</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Black Duke, The Black Duke</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick William, Duke of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, known as the Black Duke, lost his lands when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> absorbed them into the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> in 1807. In 1809 he raised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-brunswickers">Black Brunswickers</a>, a volunteer corps in black uniforms, fought his way across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and served with the British in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a>. Restored in 1813, he was killed at Quatre Bras in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-william-duke-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel.jpg" alt="Frederick William, Duke of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Christian August Schwartz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herzog_Friedrich_Wilhelm_von_Braunschweig-Oels,_der_Schwarze_Herzog.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
